### 2. Parameter optimization method: (search\_method)

##### Subsample Ratio (subsample)

Default value: 1, range is 0.1-1, one decimal place, user can customize the parameter

Hint: Controls the proportion of sample data used for each tree training **to reduce overfitting and improve the model generalization ability.&#x20;**&#x20;

##### Proportion of feature sub - samples used for each tree (colsample\_b)&#x20;

The default value is 1, the range is 0 to 1, one decimal place, and the user can customize the parameters&#x20;

Hint: What percentage of features are randomly selected for each tree to build the model during training?

##### Minimum loss reduction (gamma)

The default value is 0, and the user can customize it to one decimal place from 0 to 10&#x20;

##### Minimum loss reduction (gamma)

The default value is 0, and the user can customize it to one decimal place from 0 to 10&#x20;

Hint: gamma control **whether to prune&#x20;**. It is a regularization parameter used to specify the minimum loss function drop required for node splitting. Only when the&#x20;**&#x20;reduction of the loss function&#x20;**&#x20;brought by the split is greater than gamma, the node will be split. A larger gamma value will make the model more conservative, thus preventing overfitting; while a smaller value will make the model easier to split nodes, which may improve the performance of the training dataset but increase the risk of overfitting.&#x20;

##### &#x20;L1 regularization coefficient - (reg\_alpha)&#x20;

The default value is 0. The user can choose 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0, 1, 10, or customize it to three decimal places from 0 to 100&#x20;

Hint: Add **L1 penalty&#x20;**&#x74;o the leaf weights of the model to force the weights of some features to be 0

Hint: Add **L1 penalty&#x20;**&#x74;o the leaf weights of the model to force the weights of some features to be 0

##### L2 regularization coefficient - l2 (reg\_lambda)&#x20;

The default value is 1. The user can choose 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0, 1, 10, or customize it to three decimal places from 0 to 100&#x20;

Hint:**Add L2 penalty to the leaf weights of the model to prevent overfitting caused by excessive weights.**



