## Usage Guide:&#x20;

1. Application Introduction&#x20;

This application provides a simple and intuitive way to use the Naive Bayesian Model for Machine Learning modeling. The Naive Bayesian Model is a class of probabilistic classification models based on Bayes' theorem and assuming that features are independent of each other. It has the advantages of high computational efficiency, friendliness to small data sets, and strong robustness to noise. This algorithm is suitable for tasks such as text classification, junk email recognition, and sentiment analysis. This application supports users to import data, train models, adjust parameters, and visually analyze model performance.&#x20;

###

* Function Overview

###### ● Data Import

Support the upload and preview of common data formats such as CSV, XLSX, XLS, TXT, etc.&#x20;

###### ● Model Training

###

* Function Overview

###### ● Data Import

Support the upload and preview of common data formats such as CSV, XLSX, XLS, TXT, etc.&#x20;

###### ● Model Training

Use the Naive Bayesian Model for classification modeling, supporting multiple common variants, including:&#x20;

* Gaussian Naive Bayesian Model (GaussianNB): suitable for data with continuous variables as features.&#x20;

* Multinomial Naive Bayesian Model (MultinomialNB): Suitable for discrete data where features are counts or frequencies, such as text classification.

###### ● Hyperparameter Tuning

The following core parameters can be adjusted:&#x20;

● var\_smoothing (for GaussianNB)

A small constant used to stabilize the algorithm, preventing division by zero errors and adjusting the model's sensitivity to variance.&#x20;

* Default value: 1e-9

● alpha (for MultinomialNB and BernoulliNB)

Laplace smoothing parameter to avoid zero probability problems.&#x20;

* Default value: 1.0

● fit\_prior (all models)

* Default value: 1e-9

● alpha (for MultinomialNB and BernoulliNB)

Laplace smoothing parameter to avoid zero probability problems.&#x20;

* Default value: 1.0

● fit\_prior (all models)

Whether to learn class prior probabilities. If false, a uniform prior will be used.&#x20;

* Default value: True

● class\_prior (all models)

Prior probabilities of classes. If specified the priors are not adjusted according to the data.&#x20;

* Default: None

###### ● Model Evaluation

Support evaluation metrics such as cross - validation, accuracy, F1 score, confusion matrix, classification report, ROC curve, etc., to help comprehensively understand the model performance.&#x20;

###### ● Feature analysis

Although the Naive Bayesian Model itself does not provide feature importance scores, the decision basis of the model can be understood indirectly by analyzing the conditional probability under each category or the contribution strength of features to classification.&#x20;

