### User Guide:&#x20;

* Application Introduction&#x20;

This application provides an efficient and easy - to - use way to perform feature selection, based on the&#x20;**&#x20;Boruta&#x20;**&#x20;algorithm, which is a feature selection method based on random forest. Boruta iteratively determines the importance of each feature and finally selects the features that have a significant impact on the target variable. It is especially suitable for tasks with high feature dimensions and can significantly reduce redundant features before model training, improving the accuracy and interpretability of the model.&#x20;

The Boruta algorithm evaluates the importance of each feature by constructing "shadows" of the features and comparing them to the performance of the actual features. It automatically handles the uncertainty in the feature selection process and generates a final feature ranking to help users focus on the features that are most helpful to the model.

This application supports users to import data, set hyperparameters for Boruta feature selection, and visualize the feature selection results.&#x20;

* Function Overview

###### ● Data Import

Support the upload and preview of multiple common data formats (CSV, XLSX, XLS, TXT), and automatically detect missing values and standardize data.&#x20;

###### ● Feature selection

The application supports the use of the&#x20;**&#x20;Boruta&#x20;**&#x20;algorithm for feature selection, which helps users screen the most important features through the following steps:&#x20;

* **Range of values**: Usually 100 or 200. A larger number of iterations can improve accuracy but is computationally more expensive.

* **alpha**

  * **Description&#x20;**: Set the significance level (p-value threshold) to determine which features are meaningful. A lower alpha value means a stricter feature selection criterion, and only the most significant features will be retained.&#x20;

  * **Value range&#x20;**: Common values are 0.01 or 0.05. A smaller alpha will result in more features being removed.&#x20;



###### ● Model Evaluation and Visualization

After feature selection, users can use the following evaluation methods to further analyze the effectiveness of Boruta:&#x20;

* **Feature Importance Plot**:&#x20;

  * Visualize the importance of each feature to help users understand the impact of each feature on the target variable.&#x20;

* **Model Training and Evaluation**:&#x20;

* Visualize the importance of each feature to help users understand the impact of each feature on the target variable.&#x20;

* **Model Training and Evaluation**:&#x20;

  * Use models such as random forest to train and evaluate the data set after feature selection.

  * Support model evaluation methods such as cross - validation, learning curve, mean squared error (MSE), etc.&#x20;

* **ROC Curve and Confusion Matrix**:&#x20;

  * Automatically generate ROC curves and confusion matrices during model training and evaluation to help evaluate the performance of classification models.&#x20;

* **SHAP Interpretability**:&#x20;

  * Analyze the interpretability of the model through SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) and evaluate the contribution of each feature to the prediction results.&#x20;

* **Learning curve after feature selection**:&#x20;

* **Learning curve after feature selection**:&#x20;

  * Visualize the learning curve of the trained model after feature selection to help observe the performance changes on the training dataset and test set.&#x20;



