##### Visualization function&#x20;

###### t-SNE projection map (2D / 3D)

&#x20;Show the distribution of samples in the low-dimensional space, and support coloring different categories according to the specified label column, so as to facilitate the observation of clusters and category distribution.

Example: This is the visualization of the iris data after dimensionality reduction using the&#x20;**&#x20;t-SNE algorithm&#x20;**, which "compresses" the high-dimensional features into a 2D space. There are three things to note:&#x20;

###### 3.1. Axis: “compressed 2D space”

* **Horizontal axis (t-SNE Dimension 1), vertical axis (t-SNE Dimension 2):&#x20;**

t-SNE compresses multiple features of the original data source (such as petal length, sepal width, etc.) into two new "virtual dimensions". These two dimensions have no actual physical meaning and are only used to show the distribution relationship of samples on a plane (similar to flattening a three-dimensional object onto paper while retaining the relative positions).&#x20;

###### 3.2. Colors and legends: “Classification of different features / samples”

* **Colors in the legend**:&#x20;

###### 3.2. Colors and legends: “Classification of different features / samples”

* **Colors in the legend**:&#x20;

The figure uses colors to distinguish three types of data (actually, the legend here may be mislabeled. The real iris dataset is classified by&#x20;**&#x20;species&#x20;**, but the figure is labeled as a feature name, but the logic is similar). Whether it is a feature or a category, points with the same color represent that they are "more similar" in the original data source.&#x20;

* **How to view&#x20;**:&#x20;

For example, the green dots (related to PetalLengthCm) clustered in the upper left corner, indicating that the petal length characteristics of these samples are "very similar"; the blue dots (related to SepalLengthCm) are alone in the lower right corner, indicating that their sepal length characteristics are very different from those of other colors.&#x20;

###### 3.3. Distribution of points: "Clustering rules of data"

* **Cluster points**:&#x20;

###### 3.3. Distribution of points: "Clustering rules of data"

* **Cluster points**:&#x20;

* Same color clustering → these samples are “highly similar” in the original high-dimensional features, and t-SNE keeps them close together after compression (e.g., the green and orange dots in the upper left corner, each clustering together). **Scattered dots**:&#x20;

Colors are scattered or single dots → These samples are “very different” in the original features and far apart after compression (e.g., the blue dots in the bottom right corner are separated from other colors).&#x20;

* References&#x20;

Van der Maaten, L., & Hinton, G. (2008). Visualizing data using t-SNE. *Journal of machine learning research*, *9*(11).

Kobak, D., & Berens, P. (2019). The art of using t-SNE for single-cell transcriptomics. *Nature communications*, *10*(1), 5416.

Wattenberg, M., Viégas, F., & Johnson, I. (2016). How to use t-SNE effectively. *Distill&#x20;*, *1&#x20;*(10), e2.



